  "dct_description_sm": [
    "<div><p><span style='font-size:16px;'>The standard data collection protocol for TRCA since 2001 has been based on parts of the Ecological Land Classification System, or ELC, for Southern Ontario, developed by the Ontario Ministry of Natural Resources (Lee et al., 1998). The ELC method classifies the landscape using an organizational framework of a nested hierarchy of scale. The broadest scale is the ELC Community Class (e.g. Forest), and the most detailed is Vegetation Type (e.g. dry-fresh sugar maple forest). A full ELC survey to the level of vegetation type is a very detailed and time-consuming process. The TRCA protocol for surveying vegetation communities strikes a balance between giving enough detail to provide meaningful descriptions of vegetation communities and the constraints of time and funding. For example, resources are unlikely to be available to pursue detailed soil analysis (as outlined in the ELC field guide) in every patch surveyed in the TRCA jurisdiction, especially in the baseline phase. A code from the ELC system is assigned to each polygon. For reference, a list of communities with their codes, scores, and descriptions is carried into the field by the surveyor. Additional vegetation types described by the TRCA since 2000 that are not found in the ELC field guide are included in this reference list.&nbsp;</span></p><div>&nbsp;</div><div><figure style='width:645pt;'><figure><figure><figure><figure><figure><figure><figure><figure><figure><figure><table style='border-collapse:collapse;' width='860' cellpadding='0' cellspacing='0' border='0'><tbody><tr style='height:24.6pt;' height='33'><td style='border-right:1pt solid black; height:24.6pt; width:645pt;' colspan='2' height='33' width='860'>ELC Attribute Descriptions</td></tr><tr style='height:30.6pt;' height='41'><td style='height:30.6pt; width:161pt;' height='41' width='215'>Field Name</td><td style='border-left-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Description</td></tr><tr style='height:31.2pt;' height='42'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:31.2pt; width:161pt;' height='42' width='215'>OBJECTID</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Number uniquely generated for each polygon entry in Arcmap. A new set of numbers is generated each time a new layer is created in Arcmap.</td></tr><tr style='height:31.2pt;' height='42'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:31.2pt; width:161pt;' height='42' width='215'>POLY_NO</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Number of the ELC community polygon assigned by field staff to manage observations in the field. Used for reference and internal purposes.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>DATE</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Date the ELC community was collected/observed.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>OBSERVER</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Name of biologist who recorded the observation.</td></tr><tr style='height:46.8pt;' height='62'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:46.8pt; width:161pt;' height='62' width='215'>CPYDENSITY</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Canopy Density – A measure of how closed or open the canopy (tallest vegetation) cover is within the community. Estimate based on following cover ranges (&lt;10%, 10-25%, 25-35%, 35-60%, &gt;60%).</td></tr><tr style='height:31.2pt;' height='42'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:31.2pt; width:161pt;' height='42' width='215'>BLOCK</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Alphabetical value indicating major separated habitat units within a site, usually delineated by major roads bisecting natural cover.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CANOPYSP1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the most prevalent species within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CPYDOM1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in CANOPSP1 within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:46.8pt;' height='62'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:46.8pt; width:161pt;' height='62' width='215'>CPYSP1_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for CANOPYSP1 – provides additional information about relative abundance, identification, or condition of species. This and all ELC species modifiers are described in TRCA (2025): Addendum 2-ELC Species Modifiers.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CANOPYSP2</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the second most prevalent species within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CPYDOM2</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence the species identified in CANOPYSP2 within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CPYSP2_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for CANOPYSP2.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CANOPYSP3</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the third most prevalent species within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CPYDOM3</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in CANOPYSP3 within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CPYSP3_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for CANOPYSP3.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CANOPYSP4</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the fourth most prevalent species within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CPYDOM4</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in CANOPYSP4 within the canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>CPYSP4_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for CANOPYSP4.</td></tr><tr style='height:31.2pt;' height='42'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:31.2pt; width:161pt;' height='42' width='215'>MDLDENSITY</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Midstory Density – A measure of how closed or open the midstory vegetation cover is within the community. Estimate based on following cover ranges (&lt;10%, 10-25%, 25-35%, 35-60%, &gt;60%).</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MIDDLESP1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the most prevalent species within the middle canopy.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLDOM1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in MIDDLESP1 within the midstory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLSP1_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for MIDDLESP1.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MIDDLESP2</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the second most prevalent species within the midstory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLDOM2</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in MIDDLESP2 within the midstory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLSP2_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for MIDDLESP2.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MIDDLESP3</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the third most prevalent species within the midstory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLDOM3</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in MIDDLESP3 within the midstory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLSP3_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for MIDDLESP3.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MIDDLESP4</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the fourth most prevalent species within the midstory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLDOM4</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in MIDDLESP4 within the midstory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>MDLSP4_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for MIDDLESP4.</td></tr><tr style='height:46.8pt;' height='62'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:46.8pt; width:161pt;' height='62' width='215'>LWRDENSITY</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Lower (Understory) Density - A measure of how closed or open the understory vegetation cover is within the community. Estimate based on following cover ranges (&lt;10%, 10-25%, 25-35%, 35-60%, &gt;60%).</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>LOWERSP1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the most prevalent species within the understory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>LWRDOM1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence the species identified in LOWERSP1 within the understory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>LWRSP1_STA</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>ELC species modifier for LOWERSP1.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>LOWERSP2</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the second most prevalent species within the understory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>LWRDOM2</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Ranking of prevalence for the species identified in LOWERSP2 within the understory.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>LWRSP2_STA</td><td style='border-lef