t;' height='83'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:62.4pt; width:161pt;' height='83' width='215'>AGECLASS</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Age classification of the ELC community. Derived from successional and chronological age for treed communities only. The five potential age classes are: Pioneer, Young, Mid-Aged, Mature, and Old Growth. Interpretations for these age classes can be found in TRCA (2023): Terrestrial Biological Inventory Data Collection Protocol Update 2023 (Table 2).</td></tr><tr style='height:46.8pt;' height='62'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:46.8pt; width:161pt;' height='62' width='215'>ELCCODE1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>4–7-character code describing the ELC community. Complete list of codes and descriptions can be found in TRCA (2025): Addendum 3-Vegetation Communities Field Reference List, based on Lee et al. (1998).</td></tr><tr style='height:31.2pt;' height='42'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:31.2pt; width:161pt;' height='42' width='215'>INCLUSION1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>4–7-character ELC code describing distinct ELC subunit too small to map within the larger ELC community. If present.</td></tr><tr style='height:15.6pt;' height='21'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:15.6pt; width:161pt;' height='21' width='215'>INCL1SP1</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the most prevalent species within the inclusion (INCLUSION1).</td></tr><tr style='height:31.2pt;' height='42'><td style='border-top-style:none; height:31.2pt; width:161pt;' height='42' width='215'>INCL1SP2</td><td style='border-left-style:none; border-top-style:none; width:484pt;' width='645'>Seven-character flora code of the second most prevalent species within the inclusion (INCLUSION1).</td></tr>